OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Constant-factor-hardness-of-directed-feedback-vertex-set-September-23-2026", "title": "Constant-factor hardness of directed feedback vertex set", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-directed-feedback-vertex-set-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "102", "family_title": "The Unique Games Conjecture and optimal approximation thresholds", "abstract_html": "Approximating minimum directed feedback vertex set within any fixed constant factor is NP-hard, even on unweighted digraphs.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every fixed real constant \\(A\\ge 1\\), it is NP-hard to approximate minimum directed feedback vertex set within factor \\(A\\) on unweighted digraphs.", "statement": "<p>**Directed feedback vertex set hardness — paper’s Theorem 1.1, with the extension in Remark 8.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">All digraphs are finite and loopless; opposite arcs are permitted unless explicitly excluded.</p><p class=\"li\">A directed feedback vertex set of a digraph \\(G\\) is a set \\(F\\subseteq V(G)\\) such that \\(G-F\\) is acyclic. Write\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{DFVS}(G)=\\min\\{|F|:G-F\\text{ is acyclic}\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(A\\ge 1\\), an \\(A\\)-approximation returns a feedback vertex set of size at most \\(A\\operatorname{DFVS}(G)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed real constant \\(A\\ge 1\\), it is NP-hard to approximate minimum directed feedback vertex set within factor \\(A\\) on unweighted digraphs.</p><p>More precisely, there is a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from an NP-hard promise problem to instances \\((G,k)\\), with \\(k\\in\\mathbb N_{&gt;0}\\), having the alternatives\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\mathrm{YES}:&amp;\\operatorname{DFVS}(G)\\le k,\\\\{}[2mm]\n\\mathrm{NO}:&amp;\\operatorname{DFVS}(G)&gt;Ak.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe polynomial and its constants may depend on \\(A\\).</p><p>As stated in Remark 8.2, the hardness also holds when opposite arcs are forbidden.</p><p>Consequently, a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm with any constant approximation guarantee exists if and only if \\(\\mathrm P=\\mathrm{NP}\\). The theorem does not identify the rate at which the necessary approximation factor grows with input size.</p>", "proof": "<p>It suffices to prove the claim for every fixed integer \\(A\\ge1\\): for any fixed real approximation factor, choose an integer at least as large.</p><p>For this integer \\(A\\), choose Proposition 3’s parameters and start from the NP-hard promise problem of the external two-to-one theorem at the resulting fixed \\(\\theta\\).</p><p>The weighted construction is deterministic and polynomial, with costs bounded by the fixed constant \\(C_A\\). Its completeness bound is at most \\(4\\), while Proposition 8 gives a soundness bound strictly greater than\n\\[\nD=10A.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(S\\) be the number of weighted vertices. Apply Proposition 9 to obtain an unweighted graph \\(\\widehat G\\) with\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\mathrm{YES}:&amp;\\operatorname{DFVS}(\\widehat G)\\le5S,\\\\{}[1mm]\n\\mathrm{NO}:&amp;\\operatorname{DFVS}(\\widehat G)&gt;DS.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe transformation is polynomial because \\(C_A\\) is fixed.</p><p>Set \\(k=5S\\). Then \\(k\\in\\mathbb N_{&gt;0}\\), the YES optimum is at most \\(k\\), and in the NO case\n\\[\n\\operatorname{DFVS}(\\widehat G)\n&gt;DS\n=10AS\n&gt;5AS\n=Ak.\n\\]</p><p>A deterministic polynomial-time \\(A\\)-approximation would return a feasible feedback vertex set of size at most \\(5AS&lt;DS\\) on YES instances. On NO instances every feasible feedback vertex set has size greater than \\(DS\\). Comparing the returned size with the known threshold \\(DS\\) would distinguish the NP-hard promise cases.</p><p>For the opposite-arc exclusion in the paper’s Remark 8.2, subdivide every weighted arc with a distinct private vertex of cost \\(D+1\\), before applying Proposition 9.</p><p>A feedback vertex set of cost at most \\(4\\) in the original graph still works in the subdivided graph: a cycle avoiding it would project to a directed closed walk, and hence to a directed cycle, in the original remaining graph.</p><p>A deletion set of cost at most \\(D\\) cannot include any subdivision vertex. Every surviving original cycle would therefore lift through its private subdivisions, preserving weighted soundness.</p><p>Subdividing every arc of a loopless digraph leaves no opposite arcs, and independent-cluster cloning preserves that property. The additional vertices have constant cost and polynomial number, so the same rounding argument applies.</p><p>Finally, the size-bounded directed feedback vertex set decision problem lies in NP, with a topological order of the remainder as certificate. If \\(\\mathrm P=\\mathrm{NP}\\), exact optimization and witness search are possible in polynomial time. Together with the hardness implication, this gives the stated equivalence.</p><p>This is the paper’s proof chain. The supplied rendering’s discrepancy in Equation (3.13), and its use of different terminal parameters in the completeness and comparison proofs, has been retained explicitly rather than silently repaired.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every fixed real \\(A\\ge 1\\), approximating directed feedback arc set within factor \\(A\\) is NP-hard on unweighted simple loopless digraphs.", "statement": "<p>**Directed feedback arc set hardness — paper’s Corollary 8.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A directed feedback arc set of a digraph \\(H\\) is a set of arcs whose deletion leaves an acyclic digraph.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\operatorname{DFAS}(H)\\) for its minimum cardinality.</p><p class=\"li\">A simple loopless digraph has neither loops nor repeated arcs.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed real \\(A\\ge 1\\), approximating directed feedback arc set within factor \\(A\\) is NP-hard on unweighted simple loopless digraphs.</p><p>More precisely, there is a deterministic polynomial reduction producing such a digraph \\(H\\) and a positive integer \\(k\\) with the promise gap\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\mathrm{YES}:&amp;\\operatorname{DFAS}(H)\\le k,\\\\{}[2mm]\n\\mathrm{NO}:&amp;\\operatorname{DFAS}(H)&gt;Ak.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe reduction from directed feedback vertex set used in the proof preserves both the optimum and approximation solutions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(G=(V,E)\\) be an unweighted loopless digraph, with\n\\[\nn=|V|,\n\\qquad\nm=|E|.\n\\]\nSplit every vertex \\(v\\) into \\(v^{-},v^{+}\\), and insert the distinguished arc\n\\[\na_v:v^{-}\\longrightarrow v^{+}.\n\\]\nFor every original arc \\(e=(u,v)\\), introduce \\(n+1\\) fresh private vertices \\(w_{e,j}\\) and paths\n\\[\nu^{+}\\longrightarrow w_{e,j}\\longrightarrow v^{-},\n\\qquad\n1\\le j\\le n+1.\n\\]\nCall the resulting unweighted digraph \\(H\\).</p><p>All private vertices are distinct, so \\(H\\) has no loops or repeated arcs, even if \\(G\\) has opposite arcs. It has\n\\[\n2n+(n+1)m\n\\]\nvertices and\n\\[\nn+2(n+1)m\n\\]\narcs, and is explicitly constructible in polynomial time.</p><p>If \\(S\\) is a feedback vertex set of \\(G\\), delete the arcs \\(a_v\\) for \\(v\\in S\\). A surviving directed cycle in \\(H\\) would alternate distinguished arcs and connector paths and project to a directed closed walk in \\(G-S\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\operatorname{DFAS}(H)\n\\le\\operatorname{DFVS}(G)\n\\le n.\n\\]</p><p>Conversely, let \\(F\\) be any feedback arc set of \\(H\\).</p><p>If \\(|F|\\ge n\\), return \\(S=V\\), a feedback vertex set of size at most \\(|F|\\).</p><p>If \\(|F|&lt;n\\), return\n\\[\nS=\\{v:a_v\\in F\\}.\n\\]\nAgain \\(|S|\\le|F|\\). Suppose \\(G-S\\) contained a directed cycle. Every distinguished arc needed to lift that cycle survives. For every original arc on the cycle, the \\(n+1\\) connector paths are pairwise edge-disjoint, so at least one survives deletion of \\(F\\). Choosing surviving connector paths lifts the original cycle to \\(H-F\\), a contradiction.</p><p>Thus the returned \\(S\\) is always a feedback vertex set, and recovery is polynomial. The two maps imply\n\\[\n\\operatorname{DFAS}(H)=\\operatorname{DFVS}(G).\n\\]</p><p>They also preserve approximation solutions: an \\(A\\)-approximate feedback arc set \\(F\\) yields\n\\[\n|S|\\le|F|\n\\le A\\operatorname{DFAS}(H)\n=A\\operatorname{DFVS}(G).\n\\]\nComposing this construction with Theorem 1 and retaining its positive integer threshold \\(k\\) gives the asserted promise gap for directed feedback arc set.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(b\\ge0\\) and \\(\\alpha>0\\), there is a finite integer \\(J(p,b,\\alpha)\\), independent of \\(|S|\\), such that every Boolean \\(f:2^{S}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\) satisfying \\(I_p(f)\\le b\\) has a Boolean junta approximant on at …", "statement": "<p>**Junta approximation at fixed dyadic biases — paper’s Corollary 2.3 and Remark 2.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For a finite set \\(S\\), identify \\(\\{0,1\\}^{S}\\) with \\(2^{S}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(p\\in(0,1)\\), let \\(\\mu_p^{S}\\) include each element of \\(S\\) independently with probability \\(p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a Boolean function \\(f:2^{S}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\), define\n  \\[\n  I_{p,j}(f)=\n  \\Pr_{R\\sim\\mu_p^{S\\setminus\\{j\\}}}\n  [f(R)\\ne f(R\\cup\\{j\\})],\n  \\qquad\n  I_p(f)=\\sum_{j\\in S}I_{p,j}(f).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A function is a junta on \\(J\\subseteq S\\) if its value depends only on \\(R\\cap J\\). Constant functions are juntas on the empty set.</p><p class=\"li\">The external input is **Friedgut’s junta theorem, paper’s Theorem 2.2**: for every \\(b\\ge0\\) and \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\), there is a finite integer \\(J_{\\mathrm{unif}}(b,\\alpha)\\), independent of \\(|S|\\), such that every Boolean \\(f\\) with \\(I_{1/2}(f)\\le b\\) has a Boolean approximant depending on at most \\(J_{\\mathrm{unif}}(b,\\alpha)\\) coordinates and having error at most \\(\\alpha\\) under \\(\\mu_{1/2}^{S}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(p=2^{-k}\\), where \\(k\\ge1\\) is an integer.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(b\\ge0\\) and \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\), there is a finite integer \\(J(p,b,\\alpha)\\), independent of \\(|S|\\), such that every Boolean \\(f:2^{S}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\) satisfying \\(I_p(f)\\le b\\) has a Boolean junta approximant on at most \\(J(p,b,\\alpha)\\) coordinates with error at most \\(\\alpha\\) under \\(\\mu_p^{S}\\).</p><p>One may take\n\\[\nJ(p,b,\\alpha)=J_{\\mathrm{unif}}(kb,\\alpha),\n\\]\nincreased to one if desired.</p><p>The approximant need not be monotone. For any fixed finite collection of dyadic biases, the maximum of these junta bounds remains independent of \\(|S|\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The assertion is immediate for \\(S=\\varnothing\\), so assume otherwise. Let\n\\[\n(X_{j,a})_{j\\in S,\\ a\\in[k]}\n\\]\nbe independent uniform bits and put\n\\[\nZ_j=\\bigwedge_{a=1}^{k}X_{j,a},\n\\qquad\nF(X)=f(\\{j\\in S:Z_j=1\\}).\n\\]\nThe \\(Z_j\\) are independent Bernoulli \\(p\\) bits.</p><p>Flipping \\(X_{j,a}\\) changes \\(F\\) precisely when all the other \\(k-1\\) bits of its block are one and coordinate \\(j\\) is pivotal for the values of the other blocks. These two events are independent. Therefore, as in the paper’s Equation (2.2),\n\\[\nI_{1/2}(F)\n=k2^{-(k-1)}I_p(f)\n=2kpI_p(f)\n\\le kb.\n\\]\nBy the external uniform junta theorem, there is a Boolean approximant \\(H\\) to \\(F\\), with error at most \\(\\alpha\\), using at most \\(J_{\\mathrm{unif}}(kb,\\alpha)\\) individual bits \\(X_{j,a}\\). Let \\(J\\subseteq S\\) be the set of blocks touched by those bits. Then\n\\[\n|J|\\le J_{\\mathrm{unif}}(kb,\\alpha).\n\\]</p><p>Reveal all bits in every block indexed by \\(J\\). Since \\(H\\) is measurable with respect to these revealed blocks, the Boolean predictor with smallest conditional error from the revealed blocks has total error at most that of \\(H\\). This predictor outputs one when the conditional probability of \\(F=1\\) is at least \\(1/2\\), with any fixed convention at equality.</p><p>The unrevealed blocks remain independent of the revealed blocks, and the dependence of \\(F\\) on a revealed block is only through its AND value. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\Pr[F=1\\mid(X_{j,a})_{j\\in J,\\ a\\in[k]}]\n=\n\\Pr[f(\\{j:Z_j=1\\})=1\\mid(Z_j)_{j\\in J}].\n\\]\nThus the optimal predictor is a Boolean function only of \\((Z_j)_{j\\in J}\\). Regard it as a function \\(\\widetilde f:2^{S}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\) on the original coordinates. The induced input law is \\(\\mu_p^{S}\\), so\n\\[\n\\Pr_{R\\sim\\mu_p^{S}}[f(R)\\ne\\widetilde f(R)]\\le\\alpha.\n\\]\nThis proves the asserted bound.</p><p>The proof does not require a monotone approximant. Taking a maximum over finitely many fixed biases preserves independence from the alphabet size.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are an integer \\(N\\ge L\\) and a rational probability distribution on increasing maps \\( \\psi:[L]\\hookrightarrow[N] \\) such that, for all \\(I,I'\\in\\binom{[L]}3\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Rank spreading — paper’s Lemma 4.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For a finite ordered set \\(S\\), write \\(\\binom S3\\) for its three-element subsets, represented in increasing order.</p><p class=\"li\">For probability measures \\(\\mu,\\mu'\\) on a common finite space \\(\\Omega\\), use\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{TV}(\\mu,\\mu')\n  =\\max_{A\\subseteq\\Omega}|\\mu(A)-\\mu'(A)|.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L\\ge3\\) be an integer and let \\(\\eta&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The external combinatorial inputs are the finite Ramsey theorem for triples and the finite minimax theorem.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are an integer \\(N\\ge L\\) and a rational probability distribution on increasing maps\n\\[\n\\psi:[L]\\hookrightarrow[N]\n\\]\nsuch that, for all \\(I,I'\\in\\binom{[L]}3\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{TV}\\bigl(\\mathcal L(\\psi(I)),\\mathcal L(\\psi(I'))\\bigr)\\le\\eta.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\mathcal L\\) denotes the law of a random variable, and all these laws use the same random map \\(\\psi\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a positive number \\(\\delta&lt;\\eta/2\\). Let\n\\[\n\\mathcal I=\\binom{[L]}3.\n\\]\nPartition \\([0,1]\\) into a finite number \\(b\\) of intervals, each of diameter at most \\(\\delta\\), assigning boundary points to one of their adjacent intervals.</p><p>The finite Ramsey theorem for triples gives an \\(N\\) such that every coloring of \\(\\binom{[N]}3\\) with at most \\(b^{|\\mathcal I|^{2}}\\) colors has a monochromatic set of size \\(L\\).</p><p>Consider the following finite zero-sum game. The minimizing player chooses an increasing map \\(\\psi:[L]\\hookrightarrow[N]\\). The maximizing player chooses a triple \\((I,I',A)\\), where \\(I,I'\\in\\mathcal I\\) and \\(A\\subseteq\\binom{[N]}3\\). The payoff to the maximizing player is\n\\[\n\\mathbf1\\{\\psi(I)\\in A\\}-\\mathbf1\\{\\psi(I')\\in A\\}.\n\\]</p><p>Fix an arbitrary mixed strategy \\(\\tau\\) of the maximizing player. Let \\(w_{I,I'}\\) be its marginal probability of the ordered pair \\((I,I')\\). When \\(w_{I,I'}&gt;0\\), define\n\\[\nq_{I,I'}(Z)=\\Pr_{\\tau}[Z\\in A\\mid I,I'],\n\\qquad Z\\in\\binom{[N]}3;\n\\]\nput \\(q_{I,I'}(Z)=0\\) when \\(w_{I,I'}=0\\).</p><p>Color \\(Z\\) by the vector of intervals containing \\(q_{I,I'}(Z)\\), one interval for every ordered pair \\((I,I')\\in\\mathcal I^{2}\\). There are at most \\(b^{|\\mathcal I|^{2}}\\) colors, so there is a monochromatic \\(L\\)-element set. Let \\(\\psi\\) be its increasing enumeration. Both \\(\\psi(I)\\) and \\(\\psi(I')\\) are triples in that set, and hence\n\\[\n|q_{I,I'}(\\psi(I))-q_{I,I'}(\\psi(I'))|\\le\\delta.\n\\]\nThe expected payoff against \\(\\tau\\) is therefore at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{I,I'\\in\\mathcal I}\nw_{I,I'}\n\\bigl(q_{I,I'}(\\psi(I))-q_{I,I'}(\\psi(I'))\\bigr)\n\\le\\delta.\n\\]</p><p>The number \\(N\\) was chosen from a bound on the number of colors that does not depend on \\(\\tau\\). Thus every mixed strategy of the maximizing player has a pure response with payoff at most \\(\\delta\\).</p><p>By finite minimax, there is a probability distribution \\(\\mu\\) on increasing maps for which the expected payoff against every pure test is at most \\(\\delta\\). Choose a rational probability distribution \\(\\widehat\\mu\\) on the same finite space satisfying\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\psi}|\\widehat\\mu(\\psi)-\\mu(\\psi)|&lt;\\eta/2.\n\\]\nSuch distributions are dense in the finite probability simplex. Since each payoff lies in \\([-1,1]\\), this replacement changes any expected payoff by less than \\(\\eta/2\\). Every pure test consequently has expected payoff less than\n\\[\n\\delta+\\eta/2&lt;\\eta\n\\]\nunder \\(\\widehat\\mu\\).</p><p>The ordered pairs include both \\((I,I')\\) and \\((I',I)\\), and \\(A\\) ranges over all subsets of \\(\\binom{[N]}3\\). The resulting inequalities are exactly the asserted total-variation bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The paper asserts that, for each fixed \\(A\\), this construction produces a loopless digraph with positive rational vertex costs in deterministic polynomial time, with every vertex cost bounded by …", "statement": "<p>**The weighted construction, its evaluation invariant, completeness, and deterministic implementation — paper’s Definitions 3.1, 3.3–3.5, Lemma 3.2, and Propositions 3.6–3.7.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>**Source game and external hardness input.**</p><p class=\"li\">A two-to-one game has disjoint vertex sets \\(U,V\\), nonempty label sets \\(X,Y\\) with \\(|X|=2|Y|\\), and, for every edge \\(e=(u,v)\\), a total map\n  \\[\n  \\pi_e:X\\to Y\n  \\]\n  with exactly two preimages of every label in \\(Y\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Parallel constraints are allowed. The edges have a rational probability distribution \\(\\nu\\), with endpoint marginals \\(\\nu_U,\\nu_V\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A labeling consists of \\(\\ell_U:U\\to X\\) and \\(\\ell_V:V\\to Y\\). Its value is\n  \\[\n  \\Pr_{e=(u,v)\\sim\\nu}[\\pi_e(\\ell_U(u))=\\ell_V(v)].\n  \\]\n  The game value is the maximum over labelings.</p><p class=\"li\">The external hardness input is **paper’s Theorem 2.1**: for every constant \\(\\theta\\in(0,1/2)\\), there are constant alphabets \\(X,Y\\) for which a deterministic polynomial-time reduction makes it NP-hard to distinguish value at least \\(1-\\theta\\) from value at most \\(\\theta\\), with exact two-element projection fibers.</p><p class=\"li\">The paper invokes the completed imperfect-completeness two-to-one theorem through Dinur–Khot–Kindler–Minzer–Safra, Khot–Minzer–Safra, and Barak–Kothari–Steurer. It does not assume perfect completeness or degree regularity. Its rational edge law is obtained by choosing the smoothing parameter rationally and enumerating the prescribed constant-length tuples and constant-dimensional subspaces.</p><p>**Parameters.**\nFix an integer \\(A\\ge1\\), and choose constants in the following order:\n\\[\nD=10A,\\qquad\nM=128(D+1),\\qquad\np_i=2^{-(i+1)},\\qquad\nq_i=2p_i\\quad(i\\in[M]),\n\\qquad\n\\gamma=\\frac{p_M}{1000}.\n\\]\nSet \\(H=8(D+1)\\). Choose integers \\(T\\ge1\\) and \\(K\\) satisfying\n\\[\nHM\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{p_MT}{8M}\\right)\\le1,\n\\qquad\nK\\ge\\frac{M2^{T}D}{\\gamma}.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\nB_0=\\frac{D+1}{\\gamma}.\n\\]\nBy Proposition 1, choose \\(J\\ge1\\) bounding the size of a junta approximant with error at most \\(\\gamma\\) for every function of total influence at most \\(B_0\\), at every bias \\(p_i,q_i\\), \\(i\\in[M]\\).</p><p>Choose a positive rational \\(\\theta\\in(0,1/2)\\) sufficiently small that\n\\[\nK\\theta\\le1,\\qquad J^{2}\\theta\\le\\gamma.\n\\]\nApply the external source theorem at this \\(\\theta\\), obtaining fixed alphabets \\(X,Y\\), and put\n\\[\nm=|X|=2|Y|,\n\\qquad\nk_{\\max}=2^{T}m,\n\\qquad\nL=10k_{\\max}+30,\n\\qquad\n\\rho=\\min\\{\\gamma,1/m\\}.\n\\]\nChoose a positive rational \\(\\eta\\) such that\n\\[\n3\\eta L^{3}2^{m^{T}}\\le\\rho.\n\\]\nUse Proposition 2 to fix a finite \\(N\\) and a rational law on increasing maps \\(\\psi:[L]\\hookrightarrow[N]\\).</p><p>All these quantities depend only on \\(A\\). In particular, \\(J\\) is chosen before \\(m\\).</p><p>**Rank vertices — paper’s Definition 3.1.**\nFor a game vertex \\(w\\), let\n\\[\n\\Lambda_w=\n\\begin{cases}\nX,&amp;w\\in U,\\\\\nY,&amp;w\\in V,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nm_w=|\\Lambda_w|.\n\\]\nThe allowed ordered tuples are\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{t=0}^{T}U^{t}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigcup_{t=0}^{T-1}(U^{t}\\times V).\n\\]\nRepetitions are permitted. For\n\\(\\mathbf d=(w_1,\\ldots,w_t)\\), put\n\\[\n\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}=\\prod_{j=1}^{t}\\Lambda_{w_j},\n\\]\nwith the empty product a singleton. Thus \\(|\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}|\\le m^{T}\\).</p><p>For every profile \\(x:\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}\\to[N]\\), introduce a formal vertex \\(b_{\\mathbf d}(x)\\). Within a tuple, introduce\n\\[\nb_{\\mathbf d}(x)\\longrightarrow b_{\\mathbf d}(y)\n\\quad\\text{if}\\quad\nx(\\lambda)&lt;y(\\lambda)\\ \\text{for every }\\lambda\\in\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}.\n\\]\nWhenever \\(\\mathbf s w\\) is an allowed extension of \\(\\mathbf s\\), identify\n\\[\nb_{\\mathbf s}(x)\\equiv\nb_{\\mathbf s w}(x\\circ\\operatorname{proj}),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\operatorname{proj}:\\Lambda_{\\mathbf s w}\\to\\Lambda_{\\mathbf s}\\) drops the last coordinate. Take the equivalence relation generated by these identifications and retain the induced arcs. Every resulting rank vertex has cost \\(D+1\\).</p><p>The class of constant profiles of value \\(j\\) is denoted by \\(o_j\\). For \\(E\\subseteq\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}\\) and \\(1\\le a&lt;h\\le L\\), write\n\\[\nb_{\\mathbf d}^{\\psi}(E;a,h)=b_{\\mathbf d}(x),\n\\qquad\nx(\\lambda)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\psi(a),&amp;\\lambda\\in E,\\\\\n\\psi(h),&amp;\\lambda\\notin E.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]</p><p>**Wildcard vertices — paper’s Definition 3.3.**\nFix an allowed tuple \\(\\mathbf d\\), an embedding \\(\\psi\\), an ordered partition\n\\(C_1,\\ldots,C_k\\) of \\(\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}\\), with \\(1\\le k\\le k_{\\max}\\), and a star set \\(Z\\subseteq\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}\\). Empty cells are permitted. Put\n\\[\nz_\\ell=\\psi(10\\ell+10).\n\\]\nThe corresponding test has an incoming arc from every \\(b_{\\mathbf d}(x)\\) satisfying\n\\[\nx(\\lambda)&lt;z_\\ell\n\\quad\n(\\ell\\in[k],\\ \\lambda\\in C_\\ell\\setminus Z),\n\\]\nand an outgoing arc to every \\(b_{\\mathbf d}(x)\\) satisfying the strict reverse inequalities. There is no restriction on \\(x\\) on \\(Z\\).</p><p>**Prefix data — paper’s Definition 3.4.**\nAt length \\(t\\in\\{0,\\ldots,T\\}\\), draw\n\\[\n\\mathbf s=(u_1,\\ldots,u_t)\n\\]\nwith independent coordinates of law \\(\\nu_U\\). Independently draw \\(i_1,\\ldots,i_t\\) uniformly from \\([M]\\), and, conditionally on these indices, draw independent\n\\[\nP_j\\sim\\mu_{p_{i_j}}^{X}.\n\\]\nPartition \\(\\Lambda_{\\mathbf s}\\) by membership patterns\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\mathbf1\\{\\lambda_j\\in P_j\\}\\bigr)_{j=1}^{t},\n\\]\nordered lexicographically with \\(1\\) before \\(0\\). Retain all \\(2^{t}\\) cells, including empty ones, and call them \\(C_1,\\ldots,C_{2^{t}}\\). Write\n\\[\nC_{&lt;r}=\\bigcup_{\\ell&lt;r}C_\\ell,\n\\qquad\nC_{\\le r}=\\bigcup_{\\ell\\le r}C_\\ell.\n\\]\nAt \\(t=0\\), there is one cell on the singleton domain.</p><p>**Test families — paper’s Definition 3.5.**\nAll test vertices are distinct from rank vertices and from one another, and all their arcs are incident only to rank vertices. In every family, draw \\(\\psi\\) independently from its fixed law. Every positive-probability outcome gives a separate vertex, whose cost is its probability times the family’s stated mass.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Full-prefix tests have total mass \\(H\\). Draw prefix data at length \\(T\\), use its \\(2^{T}\\) cells, and set\n   \\[\n   Z=\n   \\left\\{\n   \\lambda\\in\\Lambda_{\\mathbf s}:\n   \\exists i\\in[M]\\quad\n   \\#\\{j:i_j=i,\\ \\lambda_j\\in P_j\\}\n   &lt;\\frac{p_iT}{2M}\n   \\right\\}.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Single-label tests have one family of total mass \\(|X|\\) on the big side and one of total mass \\(|Y|\\) on the small side. Choose \\(t\\) uniformly from \\(\\{0,\\ldots,T-1\\}\\) and draw prefix data at length \\(t\\). Independently draw \\(w\\) from the chosen side’s endpoint marginal, a uniform \\(j_0\\in\\Lambda_w\\), and a uniform permutation\n   \\[\n   (\\sigma(1),\\ldots,\\sigma(m_w))\n   \\]\n   of \\(\\Lambda_w\\). Use a wildcard test in tuple \\(\\mathbf s w\\), with ordered cells\n   \\[\n   C_\\ell\\times\\{\\sigma(a)\\},\n   \\qquad\n   \\ell=1,\\ldots,2^{t},\\ a=1,\\ldots,m_w,\n   \\]\n   with the prefix-cell index first, and star set\n   \\[\n   Z=\\Lambda_{\\mathbf s}\\times\\{j_0\\}.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Comparison tests have total mass \\(K\\). Choose \\(t\\) uniformly from \\(\\{0,\\ldots,T-1\\}\\), draw prefix data at length \\(t\\), and independently draw \\(e=(u,v)\\sim\\nu\\), \\(i\\) uniformly from \\([M]\\), and, conditionally on \\(t\\), a uniform \\(r\\in[2^{t}]\\).</p><p>   Generate \\(R_u\\subseteq X\\), \\(R_v\\subseteq Y\\) independently across projection blocks. In the block of \\(y\\in Y\\), draw the two \\(X\\)-bits in \\(\\pi_e^{-1}(y)\\) independently with probability \\(p_i\\). If either is one, set the \\(y\\)-bit to one. If both are zero, set it to one with probability\n   \\[\n   \\frac{p_i^{2}}{(1-p_i)^{2}}.\n   \\]\n   This probability is valid because \\(p_i\\le1/4\\). The \\(y\\)-bit has marginal\n   \\[\n   1-(1-p_i)^{2}\n   +(1-p_i)^{2}\\frac{p_i^{2}}{(1-p_i)^{2}}\n   =2p_i=q_i.\n   \\]\n   Thus the complete marginals are \\(\\mu_{p_i}^{X}\\), \\(\\mu_{q_i}^{Y}\\), different blocks are independent, and\n   \\[\n   R_u\\subseteq\\pi_e^{-1}(R_v)\n   \\]\n   holds in every outcome. These bits are fresh and independent of the prefix subsets.</p><p>   For \\(w\\in\\{u,v\\}\\), define\n   \\[\n   E_w^{r}(R)\n   =(C_{&lt;r}\\times\\Lambda_w)\\cup(C_r\\times R).\n   \\]</p><p>   **Rendered-definition discrepancy retained:** the supplied page image prints Equation (3.13) as\n   \\[\n   b_{\\mathbf s v}^{\\psi}(E_v^{r}(R_v);2,17)\n   \\longrightarrow\\mathrm{test}\\longrightarrow\n   b_{\\mathbf s u}^{\\psi}(E_u^{r}(R_u);18,3).\n   \\]\n   The notation \\(b_{\\mathbf d}^{\\psi}(E;a,h)\\) was defined only for \\(a&lt;h\\), whereas the paper’s subsequent completeness and comparison proofs use the terminal vertex with parameters \\((3,18)\\). The outline records those proofs as written below; it does not silently change the rendered definition.</p><p>All wildcard partitions have admissible size:\n\\[\n2^{T}\\le k_{\\max},\n\\qquad\n2^{t}m_w\\le2^{T-1}m\\le k_{\\max}.\n\\]\nTheir tuples and the comparison tuples are allowed, and the padding in \\(L\\) contains all rank indices used.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The paper asserts that, for each fixed \\(A\\), this construction produces a loopless digraph with positive rational vertex costs in deterministic polynomial time, with every vertex cost bounded by\n\\[\nC_A=\\max\\{D+1,H,m,|Y|,K\\}.\n\\]\nIf the source game has a labeling of value at least \\(1-\\theta\\), the constructed digraph has a feedback vertex set of cost at most \\(4\\).</p><p>The rank graph satisfies the evaluation invariant stated in Lemma 3.1 below.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 3.1 establishes the rank evaluation invariant. Lemma 3.2 gives the completeness deletion set and its cost bound. Lemma 3.3 gives the deterministic enumeration, polynomial-size and bit-length bounds, and constant upper bound on costs.</p><p>The assertions are those of the paper’s Lemma 3.2 and Propositions 3.6–3.7. The discrepancy between the rendered comparison definition and the endpoints used by the completeness proof remains as explicitly recorded in the construction and Lemma 3.2.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The assignment \\( b_{\\mathbf d}(x)\\longmapsto x(\\ell(\\mathbf d)) \\) is well-defined on rank-vertex equivalence classes.", "statement": "<p>**Evaluation invariant — paper’s Lemma 3.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the rank graph of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\ell\\) be any global labeling of the source game.</p><p class=\"li\">For an allowed tuple \\(\\mathbf d\\), let \\(\\ell(\\mathbf d)\\) be its tuple of labels, using the same global label in repeated occurrences of a game vertex.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The assignment\n\\[\nb_{\\mathbf d}(x)\\longmapsto x(\\ell(\\mathbf d))\n\\]\nis well-defined on rank-vertex equivalence classes. Every rank arc strictly increases this value.</p><p>Consequently, the rank graph has no self-loops, and \\(o_1,\\ldots,o_N\\) are distinct vertices with an arc \\(o_j\\to o_{j'}\\) whenever \\(j&lt;j'\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For every generating identification,\n\\[\n(x\\circ\\operatorname{proj})(\\ell(\\mathbf s),\\ell(w))\n=x(\\ell(\\mathbf s)).\n\\]\nThus evaluation is constant on each equivalence class. This remains true when vertices repeat in a tuple, since repeated coordinates are evaluated at the same global label.</p><p>A rank arc is strictly increasing at every tuple of labels, and hence at the particular tuple supplied by \\(\\ell\\). Its two endpoints cannot become the same equivalence class.</p><p>Finally, constant profiles identify with their representatives at the empty tuple, and their evaluated values are the distinct integers \\(1,\\ldots,N\\). The strict rank arcs between increasing constant profiles give the asserted arcs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The deletion set described below has cost at most \\(4\\), and the paper’s increasing-evaluation argument shows that the remaining graph is acyclic using those comparison endpoints.", "statement": "<p>**Weighted completeness — paper’s Proposition 3.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the parameters, costs, and test families of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose the input game has a labeling \\(\\ell\\) satisfying a fraction at least \\(1-\\theta\\) of its constraints under \\(\\nu\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The comparison portion of the proof below uses the terminal parameters \\((3,18)\\), as in the paper’s proof, rather than the \\((18,3)\\) printed in the supplied rendering of Equation (3.13).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The deletion set described below has cost at most \\(4\\), and the paper’s increasing-evaluation argument shows that the remaining graph is acyclic using those comparison endpoints.</p>", "proof": "<p>Delete every wildcard test whose evaluated tuple \\(\\ell(\\mathbf d)\\) lies in its star set, and every comparison test whose edge is not satisfied by \\(\\ell\\). Denote this deletion set by \\(F_{\\mathrm{yes}}\\). No rank vertex is deleted.</p><p>First bound its cost. In a full-prefix test, conditional on any fixed vertex tuple \\(\\mathbf s\\), the count\n\\[\n\\#\\{j:i_j=i,\\ \\ell(u_j)\\in P_j\\}\n\\]\nis binomial with mean\n\\[\n\\mu_i=\\frac{p_iT}{M}.\n\\]\nIndeed, indices and subsets are independent between slots, so each slot contributes an independent Bernoulli variable of parameter \\(p_i/M\\). This also applies when the same game vertex appears in several slots.</p><p>For a binomial random variable \\(Z\\) of mean \\(\\mu\\), exponential Markov gives\n\\[\n\\Pr[Z&lt;\\mu/2]\n\\le e^{\\mu/2}\\mathbb E[e^{-Z}]\n\\le\\exp\\bigl(-(1/2-e^{-1})\\mu\\bigr)\n\\le\\exp(-\\mu/8).\n\\]\nThe middle inequality follows from\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[e^{-Z}]\n=(1-a+ae^{-1})^{T}\n\\le\\exp(-Ta(1-e^{-1}))\n\\]\nwhen \\(Z\\) has parameters \\(T,a\\).</p><p>A union bound over the scales gives full-prefix deletion probability at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{M}\\exp(-\\mu_i/8)\n\\le M\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{p_MT}{8M}\\right).\n\\]\nMultiplication by the mass \\(H\\) gives cost at most one.</p><p>In a single-label test, membership of the evaluated tuple in the star set is exactly \\(j_0=\\ell(w)\\), of probability \\(1/m_w\\). Each of the two families therefore contributes cost one.</p><p>Comparison tests are deleted with probability at most \\(\\theta\\), and hence contribute at most \\(K\\theta\\le1\\). Thus\n\\[\nw(F_{\\mathrm{yes}})\\le1+1+1+1=4.\n\\]</p><p>To prove acyclicity, assign every rank vertex its evaluation under \\(\\ell\\), as in Lemma 3.1. Every rank arc strictly increases the assigned value.</p><p>For a retained wildcard test, let \\(C_r\\) contain its evaluated tuple, and assign the test the value \\(z_r\\). The tuple lies outside the star set. The incident-arc inequalities, evaluated at that tuple, put every incoming rank value strictly below \\(z_r\\) and every outgoing rank value strictly above \\(z_r\\).</p><p>For a retained comparison, the evaluated prefix is the same on both sides and\n\\[\n\\pi_e(\\ell(u))=\\ell(v).\n\\]\nThe coupling inclusion implies\n\\[\n\\ell(\\mathbf s u)\\in E_u^{r}(R_u)\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\ell(\\mathbf s v)\\in E_v^{r}(R_v).\n\\]\nFor the comparison endpoints used in this proof, the possible pairs of rank indices at the start and end are\n\\[\n(2,3),\\qquad(2,18),\\qquad(17,18).\n\\]\nEach pair is increasing. Assign the test any real value strictly between its endpoint evaluations.</p><p>These assignments coexist because there are no arcs between test vertices. Every surviving arc strictly increases the assigned real value, whereas a directed cycle would require a strict increase back to its starting value. Thus the remaining digraph is acyclic under the comparison endpoints used in the proof.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For each fixed \\(A\\), the numbers of formal vertices and arcs are \\(O_A(n^{T})\\).", "statement": "<p>**Deterministic polynomial construction — paper’s Proposition 3.7.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed parameters and finite rational sampling descriptions of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">The size parameter\n  \\[\n  n=1+|U|+|V|+|E|\n  \\]\n  counts parallel constraints as distinct edges.</p><p class=\"li\">Every outcome of positive probability is included explicitly, rather than sampled as a random output.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each fixed \\(A\\), the numbers of formal vertices and arcs are \\(O_A(n^{T})\\). The equivalence relation on rank vertices, outcome costs, and graph can be computed deterministically in polynomial time in the game’s encoding length. Costs have polynomial bit length and are at most \\(C_A\\).</p><p>The paper’s looplessness assertion follows from the evaluation invariant for rank arcs and the separation of test vertices from rank vertices.</p>", "proof": "<p>There are at most\n\\[\n(2T+1)n^{T}\n\\]\nallowed tuples. Each has at most \\(N^{m^{T}}\\) formal rank vertices. Every factor except \\(n^{T}\\) is constant for fixed \\(A\\).</p><p>For each prefix slot there are at most \\(M2^{m}\\) choices of index and subset. The support of the rank law, the number of alphabet permutations, and the choices of individual labels are all bounded by constants.</p><p>A full-prefix test uses \\(T\\) game-vertex slots and has \\(O_A(n^{T})\\) outcomes. A single-label test has at most \\(T-1\\) prefix vertices and one endpoint, also giving \\(O_A(n^{T})\\) outcomes. A comparison test has at most \\(T-1\\) prefix vertices and one input edge; all remaining choices, including the coupled subsets and candidate cell, range over constant finite sets. Hence it too has \\(O_A(n^{T})\\) outcomes.</p><p>Within one tuple there are at most \\(N^{2m^{T}}\\) formal rank arcs. A wildcard test has at most \\(2N^{m^{T}}\\) incident arcs, and a comparison test has two arcs. Thus the total formal vertex and arc counts are \\(O_A(n^{T})\\).</p><p>Computing the equivalence relation and discarding duplicate arcs cannot increase these counts. Lemma 3.1 rules out rank self-loops; every other arc joins a rank vertex to a distinct test vertex.</p><p>For numerical representation, the endpoint marginals are sums of input rational edge probabilities. A common denominator can be formed from the product of input denominators, whose bit length is at most their total encoding length. Thus the marginals have polynomial bit length.</p><p>Every outcome probability is a product of at most \\(T+1\\) input-dependent edge or endpoint probabilities and a fixed number of rational factors depending only on \\(A\\). Its bit length is polynomial. Zero-probability outcomes are omitted.</p><p>The rank law from Proposition 2 is a fixed finite rational table and may be hardwired for fixed \\(A\\). Alternatively, a computable finite Ramsey bound and exhaustive search over finite rational probability tables recover such a law: every total-variation constraint is an exact rational inequality, and the strict slack in Proposition 2’s proof ensures termination. This preprocessing depends only on \\(A\\).</p><p>Each outcome probability is at most one. A test cost is therefore at most its family mass, while every rank vertex costs \\(D+1\\). All costs are at most\n\\[\nC_A=\\max\\{D+1,H,m,|Y|,K\\}.\n\\]\nNo efficient dependence on a variable parameter \\(A\\) is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every fixed allowed tuple, \\( \\Pr_{\\psi}[\\mathbf d\\text{ is bad}] \\le3\\eta L^{3}2^{|\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}|} \\le\\rho. \\) The same bound holds for a tuple sampled independently of \\(\\psi\\).", "statement": "<p>**Boolean functions read from a surviving order — paper’s Definition 4.2 and Lemmas 4.3–4.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the weighted graph and parameters of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(F\\) is a feedback vertex set of cost at most \\(D\\), and fix a total topological order \\(\\prec\\) of the surviving vertices.</p><p class=\"li\">Every rank vertex survives because its cost is \\(D+1\\). In particular,\n  \\[\n  o_1\\prec o_2\\prec\\cdots\\prec o_N.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">All subsequent data are sampled after \\(F\\) and this order are fixed.</p><p class=\"li\">A test family of total mass \\(W\\) pays deletion cost exactly \\(W\\) times its sampled test’s deletion probability.</p><p class=\"li\">For an allowed tuple \\(\\mathbf d\\), a subset \\(E\\subseteq\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}\\), and \\(1\\le a&lt;c&lt;h\\le L\\), define\n  \\[\n  G_{\\mathbf d}(E;a,c,h)\n  =\n  \\mathbf1\\{b_{\\mathbf d}^{\\psi}(E;a,h)\\prec o_{\\psi(c)}\\},\n  \\qquad\n  g_{\\mathbf d}(E)=G_{\\mathbf d}(E;2,15,L-1).\n  \\]\n  Dependence on \\(\\psi\\) is suppressed.</p><p class=\"li\">A triple \\((a,c,h)\\) is usable if\n  \\[\n  5\\le c\\le L-5,\\qquad\n  2\\le a&lt;c,\\qquad\n  c+2\\le h\\le L.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The tuple \\(\\mathbf d\\) is good at \\(\\psi\\) if\n  \\[\n  G_{\\mathbf d}(E;a,c,h)=g_{\\mathbf d}(E)\n  \\]\n  for every subset \\(E\\) and every usable triple. Otherwise it is bad.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. For every fixed allowed tuple,\n   \\[\n   \\Pr_{\\psi}[\\mathbf d\\text{ is bad}]\n   \\le3\\eta L^{3}2^{|\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}|}\n   \\le\\rho.\n   \\]\n   The same bound holds for a tuple sampled independently of \\(\\psi\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(\\mathbf d\\) is good, \\(g_{\\mathbf d}\\) is monotone, with\n   \\[\n   g_{\\mathbf d}(\\varnothing)=0,\n   \\qquad\n   g_{\\mathbf d}(\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d})=1.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For every allowed extension \\(\\mathbf s w\\), every \\(E\\subseteq\\Lambda_{\\mathbf s}\\), and every increasing triple,\n   \\[\n   G_{\\mathbf s w}(E\\times\\Lambda_w;a,c,h)\n   =G_{\\mathbf s}(E;a,c,h).\n   \\]\n   Thus\n   \\[\n   g_{\\mathbf s w}(E\\times\\Lambda_w)=g_{\\mathbf s}(E),\n   \\]\n   and goodness of an extension implies goodness of every shorter prefix.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Consider a wildcard test with good tuple \\(\\mathbf d\\), cells \\(C_1,\\ldots,C_k\\), and star set \\(Z\\). For an integer \\(d\\in\\{0,\\ldots,k\\}\\), put\n   \\[\n   E=\\left(\\bigcup_{\\ell=1}^{d}C_\\ell\\right)\\setminus Z.\n   \\]\n   If\n   \\[\n   g_{\\mathbf d}(E)=0,\n   \\qquad\n   g_{\\mathbf d}(E\\cup Z)=1,\n   \\]\n   then the test belongs to \\(F\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 4.1 proves the goodness probability. Lemma 4.2 proves normalization and monotonicity on good tuples. Lemma 4.3 proves compatibility and inherited goodness. Lemma 4.4 proves the stated wildcard deletion rule.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\Pr_{\\psi}[\\mathbf d\\text{ is bad}] \\le3\\eta L^{3}2^{|\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}|} \\le\\rho. \\) The bound remains valid after averaging over any tuple distribution independent of \\(\\psi\\).", "statement": "<p>**Probability of goodness — paper’s Lemma 4.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the fixed surviving order, rank law, and definitions of \\(G_{\\mathbf d}\\), \\(g_{\\mathbf d}\\), and goodness from Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an allowed tuple \\(\\mathbf d\\), independently of the fresh embedding.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\Pr_{\\psi}[\\mathbf d\\text{ is bad}]\n\\le3\\eta L^{3}2^{|\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}|}\n\\le\\rho.\n\\]\nThe bound remains valid after averaging over any tuple distribution independent of \\(\\psi\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(E\\subseteq\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}\\). For \\(r_1&lt;r_2&lt;r_3\\) in \\([N]\\), let \\(x_{r_1,r_3}\\) take value \\(r_1\\) on \\(E\\) and \\(r_3\\) on its complement. Once the surviving order is fixed,\n\\[\n(r_1,r_2,r_3)\n\\longmapsto\n\\mathbf1\\{b_{\\mathbf d}(x_{r_1,r_3})\\prec o_{r_2}\\}\n\\]\nis a deterministic Boolean function of this triple alone.</p><p>Proposition 2 therefore gives, for any two increasing triples of positions, usable or not,\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\mathbb E_{\\psi}G_{\\mathbf d}(E;a,c,h)\n-\n\\mathbb E_{\\psi}G_{\\mathbf d}(E;a',c',h')\n\\right|\n\\le\\eta.\n\\]</p><p>This compares expectations, not disagreement under the same embedding. To control disagreement, use pointwise ordered pairs of bits, for which disagreement probability equals the absolute difference of expectations.</p><p>Fix a usable \\((a,c,h)\\), and put\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_0&amp;=G_{\\mathbf d}(E;2,15,L-1),\\\\\nA_1&amp;=G_{\\mathbf d}(E;2,c,L-1),\\\\\nA_2&amp;=G_{\\mathbf d}(E;1,c,c+1),\\\\\nA_3&amp;=G_{\\mathbf d}(E;a,c,h).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe bits \\(A_0,A_1\\) compare the same rank vertex to two constant vertices. They are pointwise ordered:\n\\[\nA_0\\le A_1\\quad(c\\ge15),\n\\qquad\nA_1\\le A_0\\quad(c\\le15).\n\\]\nHence \\(\\Pr[A_0\\ne A_1]\\le\\eta\\).</p><p>The profile of \\(b_{\\mathbf d}^{\\psi}(E;1,c+1)\\) is strictly smaller at every coordinate than that of \\(b_{\\mathbf d}^{\\psi}(E;2,L-1)\\): use \\(1&lt;2\\) on \\(E\\) and \\(c+1&lt;L-1\\) on its complement. The strict rank arc forces \\(A_2\\ge A_1\\).</p><p>Similarly, \\(1&lt;a\\) and \\(c+1&lt;h\\) imply \\(A_2\\ge A_3\\). The expectation comparison consequently gives\n\\[\n\\Pr[A_1\\ne A_2]\\le\\eta,\n\\qquad\n\\Pr[A_2\\ne A_3]\\le\\eta.\n\\]\nA union bound yields\n\\[\n\\Pr[G_{\\mathbf d}(E;a,c,h)\\ne g_{\\mathbf d}(E)]\n\\le3\\eta.\n\\]</p><p>There are at most \\(L^{3}\\) usable triples and \\(2^{|\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}|}\\) subsets. A second union bound gives the first inequality. Since\n\\[\n|\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}|\\le m^{T},\n\\qquad\n3\\eta L^{3}2^{m^{T}}\\le\\rho,\n\\]\nthe second follows. Averaging proves the assertion for independently sampled tuples.</p><p>The fixed order may depend on the entire weighted construction, including the rank distribution. The argument only requires that the order be fixed before the fresh draw of \\(\\psi\\); it does not assert simultaneous goodness of all tuples.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(E\\subseteq E'\\subseteq\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}\\), \\( g_{\\mathbf d}(E)\\le g_{\\mathbf d}(E'). \\) Moreover, \\( g_{\\mathbf d}(\\varnothing)=0, \\qquad g_{\\mathbf d}(\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d})=1. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Monotonicity — paper’s Lemma 4.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit Proposition 4’s setting.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(\\mathbf d\\) is good at \\(\\psi\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(E\\subseteq E'\\subseteq\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}\\),\n\\[\ng_{\\mathbf d}(E)\\le g_{\\mathbf d}(E').\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\ng_{\\mathbf d}(\\varnothing)=0,\n\\qquad\ng_{\\mathbf d}(\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d})=1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Compare\n\\[\nb_{\\mathbf d}^{\\psi}(E';2,17)\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nb_{\\mathbf d}^{\\psi}(E;3,18).\n\\]\nThe first profile is strictly smaller everywhere. The index pairs on\n\\(E\\), \\(E'\\setminus E\\), and \\(\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}\\setminus E'\\) are, respectively,\n\\[\n(2,3),\\qquad(2,18),\\qquad(17,18).\n\\]\nThe rank arc implies\n\\[\nG_{\\mathbf d}(E';2,15,17)\n\\ge\nG_{\\mathbf d}(E;3,15,18).\n\\]\nBoth triples are usable, so goodness identifies these bits with\n\\(g_{\\mathbf d}(E')\\) and \\(g_{\\mathbf d}(E)\\).</p><p>For the extreme sets, the reference profile is \\(o_{\\psi(L-1)}\\) when \\(E=\\varnothing\\), and \\(o_{\\psi(2)}\\) when \\(E=\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}\\). Their positions relative to \\(o_{\\psi(15)}\\) give the claimed values.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(E\\subseteq\\Lambda_{\\mathbf s}\\) and every increasing triple, \\( G_{\\mathbf s w}(E\\times\\Lambda_w;a,c,h) = G_{\\mathbf s}(E;a,c,h). \\) In particular, \\( g_{\\mathbf s w}(E\\times\\Lambda_w)=g_{\\mathbf s}(E). \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Prefix compatibility — paper’s Lemma 4.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit Proposition 4’s setting.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathbf s w\\) be an allowed extension of \\(\\mathbf s\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(E\\subseteq\\Lambda_{\\mathbf s}\\) and every increasing triple,\n\\[\nG_{\\mathbf s w}(E\\times\\Lambda_w;a,c,h)\n=\nG_{\\mathbf s}(E;a,c,h).\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\ng_{\\mathbf s w}(E\\times\\Lambda_w)=g_{\\mathbf s}(E).\n\\]\nIf \\(\\mathbf s w\\) is good, then \\(\\mathbf s\\) is good. The same conclusions hold after dropping several final slots.</p>", "proof": "<p>The profile on \\(\\Lambda_{\\mathbf s w}\\) that takes value \\(\\psi(a)\\) on \\(E\\times\\Lambda_w\\) and \\(\\psi(h)\\) elsewhere is the pullback of the corresponding profile on \\(\\Lambda_{\\mathbf s}\\).</p><p>The rank identifications make the two rank vertices identical. Their comparisons to the same constant marker are therefore identical, proving the displayed equality.</p><p>Apply the identity to the reference triple and to every usable triple. Goodness of the extension gives goodness of the shorter tuple for each \\(E\\). Iteration proves the assertion for several dropped slots.</p><p>The projection identity holds on the formal product alphabets even when a game vertex occurs in more than one slot.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\( g_{\\mathbf d}(E)=0, \\qquad g_{\\mathbf d}(E\\cup Z)=1, \\) then \\(v_{\\mathrm{test}}\\in F\\).", "statement": "<p>**Wildcard forcing — paper’s Lemma 4.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit Proposition 4’s fixed feedback vertex set and order.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(v_{\\mathrm{test}}\\) be a wildcard test with tuple \\(\\mathbf d\\), embedding \\(\\psi\\), ordered cells \\(C_1,\\ldots,C_k\\), and star set \\(Z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(\\mathbf d\\) is good.</p><p class=\"li\">For an integer \\(d\\) with \\(0\\le d\\le k\\), put\n  \\[\n  E=\\left(\\bigcup_{\\ell=1}^{d}C_\\ell\\right)\\setminus Z.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If\n\\[\ng_{\\mathbf d}(E)=0,\n\\qquad\ng_{\\mathbf d}(E\\cup Z)=1,\n\\]\nthen \\(v_{\\mathrm{test}}\\in F\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(c=10d+15\\), and define\n\\[\nv^{-}=b_{\\mathbf d}^{\\psi}(E;2,c+2),\n\\qquad\nv^{+}=b_{\\mathbf d}^{\\psi}(E\\cup Z;c-2,L-1).\n\\]</p><p>If \\(\\lambda\\in C_\\ell\\setminus Z\\) with \\(\\ell\\le d\\), the two profiles take values \\(\\psi(2)\\) and \\(\\psi(c-2)\\). The inequalities\n\\[\n2&lt;10\\ell+10\\le10d+10&lt;c-2\n\\]\nput these values strictly below and strictly above \\(z_\\ell\\).</p><p>If \\(\\ell&gt;d\\), then \\(\\lambda\\notin E\\cup Z\\), so the profiles take values \\(\\psi(c+2)\\) and \\(\\psi(L-1)\\). Here\n\\[\nc+2=10d+17\n&lt;10\\ell+10\n\\le10k_{\\max}+10\n=L-20\n&lt;L-1.\n\\]\nThus the graph contains\n\\[\nv^{-}\\longrightarrow v_{\\mathrm{test}}\\longrightarrow v^{+}.\n\\]\nEmpty cells impose no conditions. The first case is absent at \\(d=0\\), and the second at \\(d=k\\).</p><p>Since \\(0\\le d\\le k\\le k_{\\max}\\),\n\\[\n15\\le c\\le L-15.\n\\]\nBoth \\((2,c,c+2)\\) and \\((c-2,c,L-1)\\) are usable.</p><p>Goodness and the assumed function values show that \\(v^{-}\\) does not precede \\(o_{\\psi(c)}\\), whereas \\(v^{+}\\) does. If the test survived, the displayed path would give\n\\[\nv^{-}\\prec v_{\\mathrm{test}}\\prec v^{+}\\prec o_{\\psi(c)},\n\\]\na contradiction. Hence the test is deleted.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "These definitions are valid even when some cells are empty or some game vertices repeat in the prefix.", "statement": "<p>**Shared prefix conditioning and average acceptance — paper’s Definition 5.1 and Proposition 5.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the graph and parameters of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a feedback vertex set \\(F\\) of cost at most \\(D\\) and a total topological order of its surviving graph, as in Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Draw \\(\\psi\\) from the rank-spreading law. Independently choose \\(t\\) uniformly from \\(\\{0,\\ldots,T-1\\}\\), and draw prefix data at length \\(t\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Denote the shared data by\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal D=\n  \\bigl(\\psi,t,\\mathbf s,(i_j,P_j)_{j=1}^{t}\\bigr).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Independently draw a fresh edge \\(e=(u,v)\\sim\\nu\\). Unless another experiment is specified, endpoint probabilities and expectations use this joint law of \\((\\mathcal D,e)\\). In particular, \\(u\\sim\\nu_U\\) and \\(v\\sim\\nu_V\\) independently of \\(\\mathcal D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For each game vertex \\(w\\), fix a label \\(\\lambda_w^\\circ\\in\\Lambda_w\\), independently of all sampled data.</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(\\mathbf s\\) is good, define\n  \\[\n  r_*=\\min\\{r:g_{\\mathbf s}(C_{\\le r})=1\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define, for every \\(w\\in U\\cup V\\),\n  \\[\n  f_w(R)=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  g_{\\mathbf s w}\\bigl(E_w^{r_*}(R)\\bigr),\n     &amp;\\mathbf s w\\text{ is good},\\\\\n  \\mathbf1\\{\\lambda_w^\\circ\\in R\\},\n     &amp;\\text{otherwise},\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]\n  where\n  \\[\n  E_w^{r}(R)\n  =(C_{&lt;r}\\times\\Lambda_w)\\cup(C_r\\times R).\n  \\]\n  Write \\(f_{w,\\mathcal D}\\) when displaying dependence on shared data.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0&lt;p&lt;1\\), put\n  \\[\n  h_w(p)=\\mathbb E_{R\\sim\\mu_p^{\\Lambda_w}}f_w(R).\n  \\]\n  This expectation is taken with the function fixed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These definitions are valid even when some cells are empty or some game vertices repeat in the prefix.</p><p>For every realization of \\(\\mathcal D\\), each \\(f_w\\) is monotone, with\n\\[\nf_w(\\varnothing)=0,\n\\qquad\nf_w(\\Lambda_w)=1.\n\\]\nThe entire family \\((f_w)_{w\\in U\\cup V}\\) is fixed by \\(\\mathcal D\\), without using the fresh edge or a fresh scale index.</p><p>Under this same law of \\((\\mathcal D,e)\\), for every \\(i\\in[M]\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E h_u(p_i)\\ge\\frac{p_i}{4}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First verify the definitions. On a good tuple, the cumulative-set bits are monotone, start at \\(0\\), and end at \\(1\\). There is a unique transition, characterized by\n\\[\ng_{\\mathbf s}(C_{&lt;r_*})=0,\n\\qquad\ng_{\\mathbf s}(C_{\\le r_*})=1.\n\\]\nThe transition cell is nonempty because an empty cell cannot change a bit.</p><p>If \\(\\mathbf s w\\) is good, its prefix is good by Proposition 4, so \\(r_*\\) is defined. If \\(\\mathbf s\\) is bad, every extension is bad and all functions use the fallback.</p><p>For a good extension, compatibility gives\n\\[\nf_w(\\varnothing)=g_{\\mathbf s}(C_{&lt;r_*})=0,\n\\qquad\nf_w(\\Lambda_w)=g_{\\mathbf s}(C_{\\le r_*})=1.\n\\]\nSince \\(E_w^{r_*}(R)\\) increases with \\(R\\), \\(f_w\\) is monotone. The fallback has the same properties. The family is determined before a fresh edge or scale is drawn.</p><p>The marginal goodness bounds used below are\n\\[\n\\Pr[\\mathbf s u\\text{ is bad}]\\le\\rho,\n\\qquad\n\\Pr[\\mathbf s v\\text{ is bad}]\\le\\rho.\n\\]\nIndeed, condition on the sampled tuple and apply Proposition 4, since the tuple is independent of \\(\\psi\\). This does not assert simultaneous goodness of all extensions.</p><p>Now consider the full-prefix experiment, with probability and expectation denoted by \\(\\Pr_{\\mathrm{full}}\\), \\(\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{full}}\\). Let \\(Z_T\\) be its star set, and let \\(\\mathcal A\\) be the event that \\(\\mathbf s_T\\) is good and its transition cell is not starred.</p><p>The star predicate is constant on each full cell, since every tuple in that cell has the same membership vector and hence the same scale counts.</p><p>Suppose \\(\\mathbf s_T\\) is good and its transition cell is starred. At the cut immediately before that cell, put\n\\[\nE=C_{&lt;\\tau_T}^{(T)}\\setminus Z_T.\n\\]\nThis is the union of the nonstar portions of the cells before the cut. It is contained in \\(C_{&lt;\\tau_T}^{(T)}\\), whose bit is \\(0\\). Moreover,\n\\[\nE\\cup Z_T\\supseteq C_{\\le\\tau_T}^{(T)},\n\\]\nwhose bit is \\(1\\): adjoining stars restores earlier starred cells and includes the entire starred transition cell. Monotonicity gives\n\\[\ng_{\\mathbf s_T}(E)=0,\n\\qquad\ng_{\\mathbf s_T}(E\\cup Z_T)=1.\n\\]\nProposition 4’s wildcard forcing rule deletes this test.</p><p>The full-prefix family has mass \\(H\\), so its deletion probability is at most \\(D/H\\). Its tuple is independent of the embedding, and its badness probability is at most \\(\\rho\\). Hence, as in Equation (5.6),\n\\[\n\\Pr_{\\mathrm{full}}(\\mathcal A)\n\\ge1-\\rho-\\frac DH\n\\ge\\frac12.\n\\]\nThe rendered proof uses the parameter bounds\n\\(\\rho\\le\\gamma\\le1/40000\\) and \\(D/H&lt;1/8\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(i\\in[M]\\), define\n\\[\nY_i=\n\\sum_{j=1}^{T}\n\\mathbf1\\{i_j=i\\}\nf_{u_j,\\mathcal D_{j-1}}(P_j).\n\\]\nOn \\(\\mathcal A\\), the transition cell is nonstarred. Its defining count condition and Lemma 5.1 imply\n\\[\nY_i\\ge\\frac{p_iT}{2M}.\n\\]\nOn every other outcome \\(Y_i\\ge0\\), including outcomes using fallback functions. Therefore\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{full}}Y_i\\ge\\frac{p_iT}{4M}.\n\\]</p><p>Calculate this expectation without conditioning on \\(\\mathcal A\\). For each \\(j\\), condition on the embedding, all data in the first \\(j-1\\) slots, and the new vertex \\(u_j\\). The function \\(f_{u_j,\\mathcal D_{j-1}}\\), including its goodness test or fallback, is determined independently of the fresh \\(i_j,P_j\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{full}}\n\\left[\n\\mathbf1\\{i_j=i\\}f_{u_j,\\mathcal D_{j-1}}(P_j)\n\\mid\n\\psi,(u_\\ell,i_\\ell,P_\\ell)_{\\ell&lt;j},u_j\n\\right]\n=\n\\frac1M h_{u_j,\\mathcal D_{j-1}}(p_i).\n\\]</p><p>The marginal law of these conditioning data and \\(u_j\\) is exactly the common law of \\((\\mathcal D,u)\\) conditional on \\(t=j-1\\): the new vertex has law \\(\\nu_U\\) independently of the earlier slots. Hence\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{full}}Y_i\n&amp;=\n\\frac1M\\sum_{j=1}^{T}\n\\mathbb E[h_u(p_i)\\mid t=j-1]\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac TM\\mathbb E h_u(p_i).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nCombining the two estimates proves\n\\[\n\\mathbb E h_u(p_i)\\ge p_i/4.\n\\]</p><p>The shared distribution has not changed with \\(i\\). The favorable full-chain event was used only to lower-bound a nonnegative sum, not to condition the expectation identifying that sum with \\(\\mathbb E h_u(p_i)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The full transition cell is nonempty, and, for every \\(0\\le j\\le T\\), \\( C_{\\tau_T}^{(T)} \\subseteq C_{\\tau_j}^{(j)}\\times X^{T-j}. \\) For every \\(1\\le j\\le T\\), the membership bit of slot \\(j\\) throughout this full …", "statement": "<p>**Refinement of the transition cell — paper’s Lemma 5.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Proposition 5’s graph, fixed deletion set, order, and definition of conditioned functions.</p><p class=\"li\">Change the sampling experiment to prefix data of full length \\(T\\), with an independent embedding \\(\\psi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  \\mathbf s_j=(u_1,\\ldots,u_j),\n  \\]\n  and let \\(C_1^{(j)},\\ldots,C_{2^{j}}^{(j)}\\) be the depth-\\(j\\) partition. At \\(j=0\\), this is the single cell \\(\\Lambda_{\\varnothing}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(\\mathbf s_T\\) is good, and let \\(\\tau_j\\) be the transition index at depth \\(j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal D_{j-1}\\) consist of the same embedding, the first \\(j-1\\) slots and their data, and deterministic length \\(j-1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The full transition cell is nonempty, and, for every \\(0\\le j\\le T\\),\n\\[\nC_{\\tau_T}^{(T)}\n\\subseteq\nC_{\\tau_j}^{(j)}\\times X^{T-j}.\n\\]\nFor every \\(1\\le j\\le T\\), the membership bit of slot \\(j\\) throughout this full transition cell is\n\\[\n\\mathbf1\\{\\lambda_j\\in P_j\\}\n=\nf_{u_j,\\mathcal D_{j-1}}(P_j)\n\\quad\n\\text{for every }(\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_T)\\in C_{\\tau_T}^{(T)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Goodness is inherited by every prefix.</p><p>A cumulative union of cells at depth \\(j\\), lifted to the full product alphabet, is a cumulative union at a boundary of the depth-\\(T\\) partition. This follows from lexicographic ordering: every refinement of an earlier cell precedes every refinement of a later cell.</p><p>By Proposition 4’s compatibility identity, its cumulative-set bit at the lifted boundary equals its bit at depth \\(j\\). In particular, the boundaries on either side of \\(C_{\\tau_j}^{(j)}\\) have bits \\(0\\) and \\(1\\). Monotonicity places the full transition between these boundaries, proving the containment. An empty cell cannot change a cumulative-set bit, so the full transition cell is nonempty.</p><p>At step \\(j\\), the depth-\\((j-1)\\) transition cell has its membership-\\(1\\) child first and its membership-\\(0\\) child second. The cumulative union at their intermediate boundary is\n\\[\n\\bigl(C_{&lt;\\tau_{j-1}}^{(j-1)}\\times X\\bigr)\n\\cup\n\\bigl(C_{\\tau_{j-1}}^{(j-1)}\\times P_j\\bigr).\n\\]\nIts bit under \\(g_{\\mathbf s_j}\\) is\n\\[\nf_{u_j,\\mathcal D_{j-1}}(P_j).\n\\]\nThe extension \\(\\mathbf s_{j-1}u_j=\\mathbf s_j\\) is good, so no fallback is used.</p><p>The bit before both children is \\(0\\), and the bit after both is \\(1\\). Thus the transition is in the first child if and only if the intermediate bit is \\(1\\). If a child is empty, the same statement follows because its two boundary sets coincide.</p><p>The full transition refines the selected child, proving the membership-bit identity. This uses formal product alphabets and literal prefix lifts, not distinctness of game vertices. Repeated vertices occupy separate slots with independently sampled indices and membership sets.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every realization and every \\(0<p<1\\), \\( I_p(f_w)\\le B_w, \\qquad h_w(p)\\le pB_w. \\) Under the common law of \\(\\mathcal D\\) and an independent edge, \\( \\mathbb E B_u\\le D+1, \\qquad \\mathbb E B_v\\le D+1. \\)", "statement": "<p>**A bias-independent pivotality budget — paper’s Definition 6.1 and Proposition 6.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For a nonempty finite set \\(S\\), let \\(f:2^{S}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\) be monotone, with \\(f(\\varnothing)=0\\), \\(f(S)=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For each \\(j\\in S\\), let \\(\\sigma_j\\) be a uniform permutation of \\(S\\setminus\\{j\\}\\), and let \\(R_a(\\sigma_j)\\) be its first \\(a\\) elements, for \\(0\\le a\\le|S|-1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  B(f)=\n  \\sum_{j\\in S}\n  \\Pr_{\\sigma_j}\n  \\left[\n  \\exists a\\in\\{0,\\ldots,|S|-1\\}:\n  f(R_a(\\sigma_j))=0,\\\n  f(R_a(\\sigma_j)\\cup\\{j\\})=1\n  \\right].\n  \\]\n  Both empty and full prefixes are permitted. A permutation contributes only once for each label, regardless of the number of witnessing prefixes. In particular,\n  \\[\n  0\\le B(f)\\le|S|.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Return to the graph of Proposition 3, a fixed feedback vertex set \\(F\\) of cost at most \\(D\\), its fixed surviving order, and the conditioned functions and common law \\((\\mathcal D,e=(u,v))\\) of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  B_w=B(f_w)\\qquad(w\\in U\\cup V).\n  \\]\n  These variables depend on \\(\\mathcal D\\) and the named vertex, not on a fresh edge or fresh bias index.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every realization and every \\(0&lt;p&lt;1\\),\n\\[\nI_p(f_w)\\le B_w,\n\\qquad\nh_w(p)\\le pB_w.\n\\]\nUnder the common law of \\(\\mathcal D\\) and an independent edge,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E B_u\\le D+1,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E B_v\\le D+1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 6.1 gives the pointwise influence and acceptance inequalities for every conditioned function.</p><p>To prove the expectation bounds, fix either side of the game, and sample \\(w\\) independently of \\(\\mathcal D\\) from that side’s endpoint marginal. Its alphabet size \\(m_w\\) is constant on that side. This is exactly the marginal law of the shared data and endpoint in the corresponding single-label test family.</p><p>First fix \\(\\mathcal D,w\\) for which \\(\\mathbf s w\\) is good. The conditioned function uses \\(C_{r_*}\\). The remaining test randomness is a uniform label \\(j_0\\) and an independent uniform permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\Lambda_w\\). The star set is\n\\[\nZ=\\Lambda_{\\mathbf s}\\times\\{j_0\\},\n\\]\nand the cells are \\(C_\\ell\\times\\{j\\}\\), ordered first by \\(\\ell\\), then by \\(\\sigma\\).</p><p>Suppose a prefix \\(R\\) of \\(\\sigma\\) with \\(j_0\\) omitted satisfies\n\\[\nf_w(R)=0,\n\\qquad\nf_w(R\\cup\\{j_0\\})=1.\n\\]\nChoose a cut in the refined cells of \\(C_{r_*}\\) so that exactly the nonstar labels of \\(R\\) have been passed within that block. Such a cut exists because omitting \\(j_0\\) preserves the order of all other labels. For \\(R=\\varnothing\\), cut before the block; for \\(R=\\Lambda_w\\setminus\\{j_0\\}\\), cut after it.</p><p>The union of refined cells before the cut, with stars removed, is exactly\n\\[\nE=\n\\bigl((C_{&lt;r_*}\\times\\Lambda_w)\\setminus Z\\bigr)\n\\cup(C_{r_*}\\times R).\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nE\\subseteq E_w^{r_*}(R),\n\\qquad\nE\\cup Z\\supseteq E_w^{r_*}(R\\cup\\{j_0\\}).\n\\]\nThe second inclusion restores stars in earlier cells; stars in later cells only enlarge the set. Monotonicity gives\n\\[\ng_{\\mathbf s w}(E)=0,\n\\qquad\ng_{\\mathbf s w}(E\\cup Z)=1.\n\\]\nProposition 4 forces this test into \\(F\\).</p><p>The set identities remain valid with empty cells and repeated game vertices in the tuple. They use formal product domains and prefix identifications, not independence of labels in repeated slots.</p><p>For fixed \\(j_0\\), deleting \\(j_0\\) from a uniform permutation gives a uniform permutation of \\(\\Lambda_w\\setminus\\{j_0\\}\\). Averaging the forced-deletion event over \\(j_0\\) yields, on good extensions,\n\\[\n\\Pr[\\text{sampled test}\\in F\\mid\\mathcal D,w]\n\\ge\\frac{B_w}{m_w}.\n\\]\nA test is charged once if some witnessing prefix exists.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal G_w=\\{\\mathbf s w\\text{ is good}\\}\\). Since the test family has mass \\(m_w\\), its deleted cost is at least\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[B_w\\mathbf1_{\\mathcal G_w}].\n\\]\nThe total deleted cost is at most \\(D\\), so\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[B_w\\mathbf1_{\\mathcal G_w}]\\le D.\n\\]</p><p>On the complement, the fallback function remains monotone with bottom zero and top one, so \\(B_w\\le m_w\\). Proposition 5’s sampled-extension bound gives\n\\[\n\\Pr(\\mathcal G_w^c)\\le\\rho.\n\\]\nSince \\(m_w\\le m\\) and \\(\\rho\\le1/m\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[B_w\\mathbf1_{\\mathcal G_w^c}]\n\\le m_w\\rho\\le1.\n\\]\nAdding proves \\(\\mathbb E B_w\\le D+1\\) on either endpoint marginal, and hence both claimed bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( I_p(f)\\le B(f), \\qquad \\mathbb E_{R\\sim\\mu_p^{S}}f(R)\\le pB(f). \\)", "statement": "<p>**Arrival-time inequalities — paper’s Lemma 6.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\ne\\varnothing\\), and let \\(f:2^{S}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\) be monotone with bottom \\(0\\) and top \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(B(f)\\) as in Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0&lt;p&lt;1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nI_p(f)\\le B(f),\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_{R\\sim\\mu_p^{S}}f(R)\\le pB(f).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Give each \\(j\\in S\\) an independent uniform arrival time \\(T_j\\in[0,1]\\). Almost surely all times are distinct.</p><p>For each \\(j\\), let \\(\\sigma_j\\) be the arrival order of the other elements, and let \\(A_j\\) be the event that some prefix of \\(\\sigma_j\\) witnesses \\(j\\)’s pivotality. The permutation is uniform, so\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\in S}\\Pr(A_j)=B(f).\n\\]\nMoreover, \\(A_j\\) is determined entirely by the arrival times of elements other than \\(j\\), and is independent of \\(T_j\\).</p><p>For the influence inequality, put\n\\[\nR_{-j}(p)=\\{k\\in S\\setminus\\{j\\}:T_k\\le p\\}.\n\\]\nIt has law \\(\\mu_p^{S\\setminus\\{j\\}}\\) and is a prefix of \\(\\sigma_j\\). By monotonicity, the event defining \\(I_{p,j}(f)\\) is\n\\[\n\\{f(R_{-j}(p))=0,\\ f(R_{-j}(p)\\cup\\{j\\})=1\\},\n\\]\nwhich is contained in \\(A_j\\). Summing gives \\(I_p(f)\\le B(f)\\).</p><p>For the acceptance inequality, put\n\\[\nR(p)=\\{j\\in S:T_j\\le p\\},\n\\]\nwhich has law \\(\\mu_p^{S}\\). Along the complete arrival sequence, \\(f\\) starts at zero, ends at one, and never decreases. There is a unique arriving element where it changes from zero to one.</p><p>If \\(f(R(p))=1\\) and this element is \\(j\\), then \\(T_j\\le p\\), and the elements arriving before \\(j\\) form a prefix of \\(\\sigma_j\\) witnessing \\(A_j\\). Up to the null event of ties,\n\\[\n\\{f(R(p))=1\\}\n\\subseteq\n\\bigcup_{j\\in S}\\bigl(\\{T_j\\le p\\}\\cap A_j\\bigr).\n\\]\nA union bound and independence give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E f(R(p))\n\\le\\sum_{j\\in S}\\Pr(T_j\\le p,A_j)\n=p\\sum_{j\\in S}\\Pr(A_j)\n=pB(f).\n\\]</p><p>Only the possible-witness event \\(A_j\\) is asserted to be independent of \\(T_j\\); no independence of the actual switching element is needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(i\\in[M]\\), under the same common law, \\( \\mathbb E\\bigl[h_u(p_i)(1-h_v(q_i))\\bigr] \\le\\frac{p_i}{16}. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Product comparison from coupled tests and list decoding — paper’s Proposition 7.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the graph and parameters of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the source game has value at most \\(\\theta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(F\\) is a feedback vertex set of cost at most \\(D\\), and retain its fixed surviving order.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the functions \\(f_w\\), acceptances \\(h_w\\), budgets \\(B_w\\), and common law \\((\\mathcal D,e=(u,v))\\) of Propositions 5–6.</p><p class=\"li\">For each fixed \\(i\\in[M]\\), define two experiments for fresh subsets:</p><p class=\"li l2\">In the coupled experiment, \\((R_u,R_v)\\) has the comparison-test law at index \\(i\\).</p><p class=\"li l2\">In the independent experiment, conditionally on \\((\\mathcal D,e)\\),\n    \\[\n    R_u\\sim\\mu_{p_i}^{X},\n    \\qquad\n    R_v\\sim\\mu_{q_i}^{Y}\n    \\]\n    independently.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\Pr_{c,i}\\), \\(\\Pr_{\\mathrm{ind},i}\\) for their probabilities, including the unchanged common law of \\((\\mathcal D,e)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Neither experiment draws a candidate cell unless explicitly stated.</p><p class=\"li\">In the coupled experiment, projection blocks are independent and\n  \\[\n  R_u\\subseteq\\pi_e^{-1}(R_v)\n  \\]\n  in every outcome.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(i\\in[M]\\), under the same common law,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\bigl[h_u(p_i)(1-h_v(q_i))\\bigr]\n\\le\\frac{p_i}{16}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(i\\in[M]\\). For each value of \\(\\mathcal D\\), construct a junta approximant for every game vertex before drawing the fresh edge. Put\n\\[\n\\beta_{w,i}=\n\\begin{cases}\np_i,&amp;w\\in U,\\\\\nq_i,&amp;w\\in V.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]</p><p>If \\(B_w\\le B_0\\), Proposition 6 gives\n\\[\nI_{\\beta_{w,i}}(f_w)\\le B_0.\n\\]\nProposition 1 and the choice of \\(J\\) give a Boolean approximant \\(\\widetilde f_{w,i}\\) on a coordinate set \\(J_w\\subseteq\\Lambda_w\\) satisfying\n\\[\n|J_w|\\le J,\n\\qquad\n\\Pr_{R\\sim\\mu_{\\beta_{w,i}}^{\\Lambda_w}}\n[f_w(R)\\ne\\widetilde f_{w,i}(R)]\n\\le\\gamma.\n\\]\nChoose one such pair using a fixed ordering of the finitely many coordinate sets and Boolean truth tables.</p><p>If \\(B_w&gt;B_0\\), put \\(J_w=\\varnothing\\) and choose the constant-zero approximant, without asserting an error guarantee.</p><p>This assigns one list to every actual game vertex using only \\((\\mathcal D,i)\\), not the fresh edge. The functions are already fixed by \\(\\mathcal D\\). The choices may depend on the fixed game and fixed topological order, as may any labeling in a soundness argument. Repeated prefix vertices, or an endpoint also appearing in the prefix, do not change independence of the fresh edge from the entire prefix.</p><p>Lemma 7.2 gives, for each fixed \\(\\mathcal D\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr_{e\\sim\\nu}\n[\\pi_e(J_u)\\cap J_v\\ne\\varnothing\\mid\\mathcal D]\n\\le\\theta J^{2}.\n\\]\nThe bound persists after averaging.</p><p>Proposition 6 and Markov’s inequality give\n\\[\n\\Pr[B_u&gt;B_0\\text{ or }B_v&gt;B_0]\n\\le\\frac{\\mathbb E B_u+\\mathbb E B_v}{B_0}\n\\le2\\gamma.\n\\]\nThis uses the actual endpoint marginals and is an averaged bound, not a pointwise bound conditional on \\(\\mathcal D\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal A_i\\), determined by \\((\\mathcal D,e)\\), be the event that both budgets are at most \\(B_0\\) and\n\\[\n\\pi_e(J_u)\\cap J_v=\\varnothing.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\Pr(\\mathcal A_i^c)\\le2\\gamma+\\theta J^{2}.\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\((\\mathcal D,e)\\in\\mathcal A_i\\). In the coupled experiment, the big-side junta uses blocks indexed by \\(\\pi_e(J_u)\\), and the small-side junta uses blocks indexed by \\(J_v\\). These collections are disjoint and independently generated. Therefore\n\\[\n\\widetilde f_{u,i}(R_u)\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\widetilde f_{v,i}(R_v)\n\\]\nare independent under the coupling. They are also independent in the independent experiment and have unchanged marginal distributions. Their joint distributions agree in both experiments, including when a junta is constant with empty support.</p><p>Each endpoint’s conditional approximation error is at most \\(\\gamma\\) in either experiment, since both have the stipulated product marginals. Replacing the original functions by juntas changes the probability of the pattern \\((1,0)\\) by at most \\(2\\gamma\\) in each experiment. Consequently, as displayed in the rendered proof,\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\Pr_{\\mathrm{ind},i}[f_u(R_u)=1,f_v(R_v)=0\\mid\\mathcal D,e]\n-\n\\Pr_{c,i}[f_u(R_u)=1,f_v(R_v)=0\\mid\\mathcal D,e]\n\\right|\n\\le4\\gamma\n\\]\non \\(\\mathcal A_i\\). On its complement, the difference is at most one.</p><p>Integrating over the unchanged law of \\((\\mathcal D,e)\\), and using Lemma 7.1, gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr_{\\mathrm{ind},i}[f_u(R_u)=1,f_v(R_v)=0]\n&amp;\\le\n2\\rho+\\frac{M2^{T}D}{K}\n+2\\gamma+\\theta J^{2}+4\\gamma\\\\\n&amp;\\le10\\gamma.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe last inequality uses\n\\[\n\\rho\\le\\gamma,\\qquad\n\\frac{M2^{T}D}{K}\\le\\gamma,\\qquad\n\\theta J^{2}\\le\\gamma.\n\\]</p><p>The conditional pattern probability in the independent experiment is\n\\[\nh_u(p_i)(1-h_v(q_i)).\n\\]\nFinally,\n\\[\n10\\gamma=\\frac{p_M}{100}\\le\\frac{p_i}{16}.\n\\]\nThis proves the product-comparison statement, using the comparison endpoints employed in Lemma 7.1.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The paper’s comparison forcing argument gives \\( \\Pr_{c,i}[f_u(R_u)=1,\\ f_v(R_v)=0] \\le2\\rho+\\frac{M2^{T}D}{K}. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Comparison deletion charge — paper’s Lemma 7.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit Proposition 7’s fixed feedback vertex set, order, conditioned functions, and coupled experiment.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(i\\in[M]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">As in the paper’s proof, the terminal rank vertex used below has parameters \\((3,18)\\); this differs from \\((18,3)\\) in the supplied rendering of Equation (3.13), recorded in Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The paper’s comparison forcing argument gives\n\\[\n\\Pr_{c,i}[f_u(R_u)=1,\\ f_v(R_v)=0]\n\\le2\\rho+\\frac{M2^{T}D}{K}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal G\\) be the event that \\(\\mathbf s u\\) and \\(\\mathbf s v\\) are both good. The tuple choices are independent of \\(\\psi\\), so Proposition 4 gives\n\\[\n\\Pr(\\mathcal G^c)\\le2\\rho.\n\\]\nOn \\(\\mathcal G\\), both conditioned functions use the same prefix transition index \\(r_*\\).</p><p>Suppose \\(\\mathcal G\\) holds and\n\\[\nf_u(R_u)=1,\n\\qquad\nf_v(R_v)=0.\n\\]\nAdd the candidate-cell draw and consider \\(r=r_*\\). The start and end vertices used in the proof are\n\\[\nb_{\\mathbf s v}^{\\psi}(E_v^{r_*}(R_v);2,17),\n\\qquad\nb_{\\mathbf s u}^{\\psi}(E_u^{r_*}(R_u);3,18).\n\\]\nThe triples \\((2,15,17)\\) and \\((3,15,18)\\) are usable. Goodness and the function values show that the start does not precede \\(o_{\\psi(15)}\\), whereas the end does.</p><p>If the comparison test survived, its two arcs would require the start to precede the test and the test to precede the end, contradicting the fixed order. Thus that comparison vertex is deleted.</p><p>For fixed \\((\\mathcal D,e,R_u,R_v)\\), the candidate draw hits \\(r_*\\) with probability\n\\[\n2^{-t}\\ge2^{-T}.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\delta_i\\) be the deletion probability of a comparison vertex with its fresh scale index fixed to \\(i\\), including the candidate draw. Then\n\\[\n\\delta_i\n\\ge\n2^{-T}\n\\Pr_{c,i}[\\mathcal G,\\ f_u(R_u)=1,\\ f_v(R_v)=0].\n\\]</p><p>In the actual family the scale is uniform, and its deleted weight is\n\\[\n\\frac KM\\sum_{j=1}^{M}\\delta_j\\le D.\n\\]\nHence \\(\\delta_i\\le MD/K\\). Combining these inequalities and adding\n\\(\\Pr(\\mathcal G^c)\\le2\\rho\\) proves the stated bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\Pr_{e=(u,v)\\sim\\nu} [\\pi_e(J_u)\\cap J_v\\ne\\varnothing] \\le\\theta J^{2}. \\) The same bound holds conditionally on any data independent of a fresh edge, provided all lists are fixed by those data.", "statement": "<p>**List decoding against game soundness — paper’s Lemma 7.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let a two-to-one game have value at most \\(\\theta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assign to every game vertex \\(w\\) a list \\(J_w\\subseteq\\Lambda_w\\), possibly empty, with \\(|J_w|\\le J\\), where \\(J\\ge1\\) is an integer.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\Pr_{e=(u,v)\\sim\\nu}\n[\\pi_e(J_u)\\cap J_v\\ne\\varnothing]\n\\le\\theta J^{2}.\n\\]\nThe same bound holds conditionally on any data independent of a fresh edge, provided all lists are fixed by those data.</p>", "proof": "<p>Independently for every vertex with a nonempty list, choose its label uniformly from that list. Give each empty-list vertex an arbitrary fixed label.</p><p>If \\(\\pi_e(J_u)\\cap J_v\\ne\\varnothing\\), at least one pair\n\\((x,y)\\in J_u\\times J_v\\) satisfies \\(\\pi_e(x)=y\\). Since \\(u,v\\) are distinct vertices, the probability of choosing that pair is\n\\[\n\\frac1{|J_u||J_v|}\\ge\\frac1{J^{2}}.\n\\]\nThus this random labeling’s expected value is at least the list-intersection probability divided by \\(J^{2}\\).</p><p>Every deterministic labeling in its support has value at most \\(\\theta\\), so the expectation is at most \\(\\theta\\), proving the bound.</p><p>For the conditional assertion, fix the conditioning data first. Independence leaves the fresh edge law equal to \\(\\nu\\), so the same argument applies.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The paper’s soundness argument rules out a feedback vertex set of cost at most \\(D\\).", "statement": "<p>**Weighted soundness — paper’s Proposition 7.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the weighted construction and parameters of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the source game has value at most \\(\\theta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The order-derived functions, common distribution, and estimates are those established in Propositions 4–7.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The paper’s soundness argument rules out a feedback vertex set of cost at most \\(D\\). Equivalently, every feedback vertex set of the constructed weighted digraph has cost strictly greater than \\(D\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(F\\) has cost at most \\(D\\), and fix its surviving order. Use the common law of \\((\\mathcal D,e)\\) from Proposition 5.</p><p>For each \\(i\\in[M]\\), let\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_i=\\{h_v(q_i)\\ge1/2\\}.\n\\]\nOn its complement,\n\\[\nh_u(p_i)\\le2h_u(p_i)(1-h_v(q_i)).\n\\]\nPropositions 5 and 7 therefore give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E[h_u(p_i)\\mathbf1_{\\mathcal H_i}]\n&amp;=\n\\mathbb E h_u(p_i)\n-\\mathbb E[h_u(p_i)\\mathbf1_{\\mathcal H_i^c}]\\\\\n&amp;\\ge\n\\frac{p_i}{4}\n-2\\mathbb E[h_u(p_i)(1-h_v(q_i))]\\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\frac{p_i}{8}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSince \\(0\\le h_u(p_i)\\le1\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr(\\mathcal H_i)\\ge p_i/8.\n\\]</p><p>Proposition 6 gives pointwise\n\\[\nh_v(q_i)\\le q_iB_v=2p_iB_v.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\mathcal H_i\\) implies \\(B_v\\ge1/(4p_i)\\), and hence\n\\[\n\\Pr\\!\\left[B_v\\ge\\frac1{4p_i}\\right]\\ge\\frac{p_i}{8},\n\\qquad i\\in[M].\n\\]</p><p>These are tail estimates for one random variable \\(B_v\\) under one fixed probability law. Neither \\(f_v\\) nor \\(B_v\\) uses the fresh scale index. The junta approximants may depend on \\(i\\), but this does not change \\(B_v\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\na_i=\\frac1{4p_i}\\quad(i\\ge1),\n\\qquad\na_0=0.\n\\]\nFor every real \\(x\\ge0\\),\n\\[\nx\\ge\n\\sum_{i=1}^{M}(a_i-a_{i-1})\\mathbf1\\{x\\ge a_i\\}.\n\\]\nIf \\(a_j\\) is the largest crossed threshold, the sum telescopes to \\(a_j\\le x\\); if no threshold is crossed, it is zero.</p><p>Taking expectations yields\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E B_v\n&amp;\\ge\n\\sum_{i=1}^{M}\n(a_i-a_{i-1})\\Pr[B_v\\ge a_i]\\\\\n&amp;\\ge\n\\sum_{i=1}^{M}\\frac{a_i}{2}\\frac{p_i}{8}\n=\\frac M{64}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHere \\(a_i=2a_{i-1}\\) for \\(i\\ge2\\), while\n\\(a_1-a_0=a_1\\ge a_1/2\\).</p><p>Since \\(M=128(D+1)\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E B_v\\ge2(D+1),\n\\]\ncontradicting Proposition 6’s bound \\(\\mathbb E B_v\\le D+1\\). Therefore no feedback vertex set of cost at most \\(D\\) can exist.</p><p>This proof uses Proposition 7, and thus retains the comparison-definition discrepancy explicitly recorded earlier rather than supplying a correction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\operatorname{DFVS}(\\widehat G) = \\min\\left\\{ \\sum_{v\\in F}c_v: F\\text{ is a feedback vertex set of }G \\right\\}. \\) Furthermore, \\( |V(\\widehat G)|\\le CS^{2}+S. \\) For fixed \\(C\\), this is a deterministic polynomial …", "statement": "<p>**Rounding and cloning — paper’s Lemma 8.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be a loopless digraph on \\(S\\ge1\\) vertices.</p><p class=\"li\">Give each vertex a positive rational cost \\(w_v\\le C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  c_v=\\lceil Sw_v\\rceil.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Replace every vertex \\(v\\) by an independent set \\(C_v\\) of \\(c_v\\) unweighted vertices.</p><p class=\"li\">Replace every arc \\(u\\to v\\) by all arcs from \\(C_u\\) to \\(C_v\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Denote the resulting graph by \\(\\widehat G\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\operatorname{DFVS}(\\widehat G)\n=\n\\min\\left\\{\n\\sum_{v\\in F}c_v:\nF\\text{ is a feedback vertex set of }G\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nFurthermore,\n\\[\n|V(\\widehat G)|\\le CS^{2}+S.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(C\\), this is a deterministic polynomial construction in the encoding size of \\(G\\) and its costs.</p><p>The following gap implications hold:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. A feedback vertex set of \\(G\\) of cost at most \\(a\\) yields a feedback vertex set of \\(\\widehat G\\) of size at most \\(S(a+1)\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If every feedback vertex set of \\(G\\) has cost greater than \\(b\\), then\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{DFVS}(\\widehat G)&gt;Sb.\n   \\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(F\\) be a feedback vertex set of \\(G\\). Delete every cluster \\(C_v\\) with \\(v\\in F\\). The remaining clusters lie over the acyclic digraph \\(G-F\\).</p><p>Lift a topological order of \\(G-F\\) by placing each cluster consecutively. Since clusters are independent, this is a topological order of the surviving vertices of \\(\\widehat G\\). The resulting feedback vertex set has size\n\\[\n\\sum_{v\\in F}c_v.\n\\]</p><p>Conversely, let \\(Q\\) be any feedback vertex set of \\(\\widehat G\\), and put\n\\[\nF_Q=\\{v\\in V(G):C_v\\subseteq Q\\}.\n\\]\nIf \\(G-F_Q\\) contained a directed cycle, every cluster over that cycle would contain a surviving vertex. Choose one surviving vertex from each cluster. All required intercluster arcs are present, so these vertices lift the original cycle to a directed cycle of \\(\\widehat G-Q\\), a contradiction.</p><p>Thus \\(F_Q\\) is a feedback vertex set of \\(G\\), and\n\\[\n\\sum_{v\\in F_Q}c_v\\le|Q|.\n\\]\nTogether, these two constructions prove the optimum identity. In particular, partial deletions from clusters cannot improve the optimum beyond the corresponding completely deleted clusters.</p><p>For every \\(v\\),\n\\[\nSw_v\\le c_v&lt;Sw_v+1.\n\\]\nTherefore, for any feedback vertex set \\(F\\),\n\\[\nS\\sum_{v\\in F}w_v\n\\le\n\\sum_{v\\in F}c_v\n\\le\nS\\sum_{v\\in F}w_v+S.\n\\]\nThe optimum identity now gives both gap implications. Equivalently, replacing costs \\(w_v\\) by \\(c_v/S\\) raises the cost of every deletion set by at most one.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n|V(\\widehat G)|\n=\\sum_vc_v\n\\le CS^{2}+S.\n\\]\nThere are at most the square of this number of arcs. Each \\(c_v\\) is computed exactly by integer arithmetic on the rational input cost. For fixed \\(C\\), all vertices and arcs can be listed in polynomial time.</p><p>Positive costs ensure that every cluster is nonempty before deletions. Looplessness ensures that independent clusters realize precisely the prescribed construction.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [2, 4, "cite"], [2, 17, "cite"], [3, 4, "cite"], [3, 7, "cite"], [3, 9, "cite"], [4, 0, "cite"], [4, 8, "cite"], [4, 13, "cite"], [4, 15, "cite"], [4, 17, "cite"], [4, 18, "cite"], [4, 20, "cite"], [5, 4, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [5, 7, "cite"], [6, 4, "cite"], [7, 4, "cite"], [8, 13, "cite"], [8, 14, "cite"], [8, 15, "cite"], [8, 18, "cite"], [8, 20, "cite"], [9, 8, "cite"], [10, 8, "cite"], [11, 8, "cite"], [11, 9, "cite"], [12, 8, "cite"], [12, 10, "cite"], [13, 15, "cite"], [13, 17, "cite"], [13, 20, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [15, 17, "cite"], [15, 20, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [17, 20, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [19, 17, "cite"], [20, 0, "cite"], [21, 0, "cite"]]});
